<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Suwałki</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Suwałki is a town in north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, in Podlaskie Voivodeship near the borders with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>. It lies in the former land of the Sudovians and grew from a settlement of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/camaldolese">Camaldolese</a> monks of Wigry in the early 18th century. It gives its name to the Suwałki Gap, the stretch of the Polish–Lithuanian border between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belarus">Belarus</a> and Russia's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/konigsberg">Kaliningrad</a> region.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/suwalki.jpg" alt="Suwałki" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sławomir Milejski · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:SM_Suwa%C5%82ki_Ko%C5%9Bci%C3%B3%C5%82_%C5%9Bw_Aleksandra_2020_(2)_(cropped)2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The name derives from the Lithuanian su- (near) and valka (creek, marsh), meaning a place near a small river or swampy area. The Czarna Hańcza river flows through the town, which lies about 30 km from the south-western border of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a>, beside the Via Baltica road that links <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warsaw">Warsaw</a> with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaunas">Kaunas</a>, Riga, Tallinn and Helsinki. The Suwałki Landscape Park and Wigry National Park lie nearby. It has among the coldest climates of the larger Polish towns, and is often called the Polish pole of cold.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Yotvingian and Prussian tribes lived in the area from the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a>. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teutonic-order">Teutonic Order</a> arrived in Yotvingia their lands were conquered and stayed largely depopulated for centuries. In 1667 John II Casimir, Grand Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a> and King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, granted the land around the future town to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/camaldolese">Camaldolese</a> monks, who built a monastery and church at Wigry. The village is first mentioned in 1688, and two years later it had just two houses.</p><p>Growth was fast. By 1700 the village was split into Lesser and Greater Suwałki, on the trade route from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grodno">Grodno</a> and Merkinė to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/konigsberg">Königsberg</a>. In 1710 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/augustus-ii-the-strong">Augustus II the Strong</a> granted it the right to hold fairs and markets, and in 1715 the order's grand master, Ildefons, gave it town rights, dividing it into 300 lots. Augustus II approved the rights on 2 May 1720 and a coat of arms showing Saint Roch and Saint Romuald. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/partitions-of-poland">Partitions of Poland</a> the area passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> in 1795, and the Wigry monastery was dissolved in 1796. Suwałki then had 1,184 inhabitants, a large part of them Jewish.</p><p>After the Congress of Vienna Suwałki belonged to Congress Poland under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a>, and in 1816 the government of the new Augustów Voivodeship was gradually moved there. It later became capital of the Augustów and then the Suwałki Gubernia. By 1857 it had 11,273 inhabitants, making it the fourth most populous town of Congress Poland. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> it was annexed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-prussia">East Prussia</a> as Sudauen. Almost all of its Jewish community of about 7,000 was deported and murdered, and the Germans ran the Stalag I-F camp for Soviet prisoners of war at Krzywólka. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-army">Red Army</a> took the town on 23 October 1944, and most of its historic buildings had survived.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Suwałki is the capital of Suwałki County and had a population of 69,206 in 2021. Its sights include the Saint Alexander Co-Cathedral, Neoclassical Kościuszko street and a cemetery complex holding Roman Catholic, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a>, Protestant, Jewish and Muslim graves. The poet Maria Konopnicka, author of Rota, is among its notable people, and a museum stands in her childhood home.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Suwałki” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Suwałki</b> Agreement of 7 October 1920 left Vilnius on the Lithuanian side, but General Lucjan Żeligowski, on Piłsudski's orders, invaded the next day and took the city.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-lithuania-1918-1940">Republic of Lithuania (1918-1940) · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>They occupied the lands around modern <b>Suwałki</b>, Grodno and southern Lithuania, close kin of the Prussians and Galindians, and were organised as a loose federation of territories led by hillfort chieftains rather than a single state.</blockquote><a href="/state/sudovians">Sudovians</a></li></ul></section>
